    "result_text": "The only actual test class is `AcceptanceTest` (the others are base/helper classes), and it passes. The full build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed the `customerCode` format to `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` with minimal structural impact:\n\n- **New file** `service/CustomerCodeGenerator.java` \u2014 a small, cohesive utility (mirroring the existing `NamesakeCounter`) that holds all the new logic: upper-cased 3-letter city prefix, 3-letter last-name prefix, and a per-city 4-digit sequence (`count of existing owners in that city + 1`). Its methods have essentially zero surrounding complexity (`WMC_other` \u2248 0).\n\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 replaced the two inline code-building lines with a single delegating call `CustomerCodeGenerator.generate(owners, owner)`. This adds no branches, so the big controller class's method keeps its existing cyclomatic complexity and I edited as few lines as possible in that high-`WMC_other` context. (Plus one import line.)\n\n- **`OwnerMapper.membershipNumber`** \u2014 needed no change. It's derived generically as `getCustomerCode() + \"-M\" + ...`, so it automatically rebuilds on the new customer-code format.\n\nThe per-city count matches city case-insensitively for robustness, and the prefix helper guards against city/last names shorter than three characters.",
